# Line endings for files that are EXECUTED BY A LINUX SHELL.
#
# This repo is developed on Windows with core.autocrlf=true, so a checkout
# rewrites tracked text files to CRLF. That is harmless for TypeScript and for
# the Python scripts BookForge spawns (CPython accepts CRLF), and it is FATAL for
# a shell script that runs inside WSL: bash reads `#!/bin/bash\r` as an
# interpreter path ending in a carriage return and answers
# "/bin/bash^M: bad interpreter: No such file or directory", and every `if`/`fi`
# in the body inherits the same stray byte.
# `eol=lf` pins these to LF in the WORKING TREE regardless of autocrlf, which is
# what the WSL side actually reads (BookForge hands wsl.exe a /mnt/c path to the
# checked-out file — it does not stage a copy).
# The .py files beside them are pinned for a narrower reason: they are compared
# against, and were copied from, the campaign originals, and a platform-dependent
# byte difference makes "is this still the same patch" unanswerable by hashing.
# The .txt beside them is requirements-narrator-runtime.txt, which is read INSIDE
# the guest twice — `pip install -r` and the installer's sed/grep that pulls the
# `# import:` annotations out of it — so a trailing CR would ride into a pip
# requirement and into a module name the doctor then probes for.
electron/scripts/higgs/*.sh text eol=lf
electron/scripts/higgs/*.py text eol=lf
electron/scripts/higgs/*.txt text eol=lf
python/narrator/tests/golden/** text eol=lf
